    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently overseeing the acquisition of a significant collection of 19th-century private manuscripts for our university archives here in Belgium. We are debating whether to implement the Dewey Decimal System or the Library of Congress Classification for this specific collection. Could you provide a technical comparison of the two systems, specifically focusing on metadata scalability and their capacity for granular organization of non-standard historical documents?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Library of Congress Classification (LCC) is generally more granular and flexible for the nuanced organization of non-standard historical manuscripts, as its alphanumeric structure allows for detailed subject subdivision. Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) is broader and more categorical, which can challenge the detailed categorization of unique, heterogeneous 19th-century private papers. For massive, evolving collections, LCC's structure often scales better by accommodating specialized, cross-disciplinary classifications required by historical research.",
      "source": "target",
