    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. My main concern with LCC is the sheer complexity of the alphanumeric codes for a small team to manage, whereas DDC feels a bit too \"tight\" for what we have. If we end up with a large number of items that don't fit neatly into any established category—basically just a pile of unclassified or \"miscellaneous\" materials—how do these two systems handle that kind of systemic disorder or lack of structure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both systems offer accommodations for disorder, but their approaches differ significantly. LCC handles ambiguity through a broader range of schedules and the ability to create unique local subdivisions within established classes, allowing for more complex, bespoke metadata. DDC, conversely, tends to push unclassifiable items toward the \"000s\" (Generalities) or highly specific marginalia, which can quickly become cumbersome and lack intuitive subject coherence. Ultimately, a hybrid approach using LCC for core structure augmented by descriptive metadata (like Dublin Core) is often best for truly disordered material.",
      "source": "target",
