    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The idea of a hybrid approach sounds practical, but I'm worried about the \"unclassified\" section specifically. In our experience, when things aren't properly categorized, they tend to get lost in the stacks or end up in these weird, chaotic pockets of the archive that are just hard to track. Is there a way to prevent that kind of \"chaos\" or total loss of sense in the collection when the classification system fails to capture the essence of the items?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To actively prevent chaos, metadata practices must decouple item tracking from rigid classification. Instead of forcing everything into a single system, maintain a robust inventory based on unique identifiers (e.g., accession numbers) that point to rich, standardized descriptive metadata. This metadata should capture context, provenance, and physical description even if the subject classification is 'Undetermined.' By relying heavily on these contextual fields, you ensure that while the subject classification may be fluid, the object itself remains fully discoverable and trackable within the archive.",
      "source": "target",
